WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.479
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.590
JORDANNA CHORD:
Welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.040
My name is Jordanna,
and this is Scott,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.530
and we're here to talk to you
about the latest innovations

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.480
in Cloud data loss prevention.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.840
First, an order of business.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.910
We'll be using the Dory app
within the Cloud Next app

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.242
to take questions, and then
at the end of the talk,

00:00:23.242 --> 00:00:24.450
we'll be going through those.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:27.120
Josh is our moderator, and he'll
be helping you through those.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.040
Anything we don't
get to today, we'll

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.070
be covering-- or responding to
you before the end of the week.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.440
So in this session, we'll
be giving a quick overview

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.720
of Cloud data loss
prevention, followed

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.460
by a review of the
latest things we've

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.680
launched since the
last time we were here.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.120
Scott will then demo
all of the new features,

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.630
and then we'll end
with your questions.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.220
So why are we here today?

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.700
For those of you
new to Cloud DLP,

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.090
it provides a collection of
tools that classify and detect

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.870
for sensitive information,
anonymize and transform

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:05.319
text and images, and measure
your protection with risk

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:06.010
analysis.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.780
Why did we build Cloud
data loss prevention?

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.230
We made DLP to help you
understand and manage

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:17.340
your sensitive data.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.940
We made DLP for your data
compliance officers, your data

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.700
engineers, your
academic researchers who

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.040
all need help to
assure compliance

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.320
with policies and regulations.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.930
And most importantly,
they need help

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.600
to protect your users' data.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.660
So how do we do that?

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.140
DLP is made up of three
pillars, the first of which

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.860
is discovery and classification.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.840
A compliance
officer whose job is

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.540
to ensure that credit
card data is protected

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.630
can use DLP to
tokenize the data.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.180
But to be really sure
they've done a thorough job,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.700
they need to conduct audits
of all the persistent data

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.220
in your company to make
sure that credit cards don't

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.520
show up anywhere surprising.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.860
DLP can help this compliance
officer find and identify

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.759
credit card data along with
many other types of PII

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:12.480
across BigQuery, Google
Cloud Storage, and Datastore,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.390
and even data not
stored in the Cloud.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.220
The findings can
then be used to take

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.550
remediation steps, such as
anonymization, which takes us

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.320
to our next pillar.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:28.150
DLP provides support to
de-identify text and images

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:31.300
in both structured and
unstructured contexts.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.700
We host a number of
transformation techniques

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.750
such as masking, deletion, and
format preserving encryption

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.470
to obscure your data while
minimizing your data loss.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.210
Here's an example of a user
combining the first two product

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:48.200
pillars.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.210
As you can see, we
have a loan application

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:54.200
with several bits of PII.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.970
On the right hand
side is what the image

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.887
and what the documents
look like after they've

00:02:57.887 --> 00:02:59.330
been run through DLP.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.640
As you can see, our
detectors have automatically

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.060
identified the phone, the email,
and the social security number

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.110
at the top.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.450
We've color coded the
findings so that viewers

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.330
might know what was redacted,
but not what exactly

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:14.060
private information was there.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.720
Treating rich documents as
images and anonymizing them

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.320
this way is a great technique
to protect your user's data.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.970
But we aren't quite
done yet, which

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.750
brings us to our next pillar.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.590
A medical researcher
is conducting a study,

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:33.260
and their ethics board has asked
that they remove all ability

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.990
to tie the secret lab
result back to the patients

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.390
before publishing
their research.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.970
They've removed all of the
identifying information--

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.670
the social security numbers
and the ages and the dates

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:45.190
of birth--

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.110
and they've left no
direct identifiers behind.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.120
But to be sure that their
patients' identity is

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.740
protected, they turn
to risk analysis.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.370
In this extremely
simple example,

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.260
we can see that
there's outliers,

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.340
and that in this case, the
job title field is really

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.190
identifying, even
though normally this

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:04.315
wouldn't be considered PII.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.420
Risk analysis help
users find data

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.730
like this
automatically-- data that

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:15.990
is actually very revealing if
joined with another data set.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.089
We provide a number
of metrics which

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.339
can increase your
understanding of the risks

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.830
that your data might
pose if it were to leak,

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.510
or if you were to
share it intentionally

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:25.427
for research purposes.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:32.590
So those are the three pillars
that make up Cloud DLP.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.590
So let me tell you a
little bit about what we've

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:35.850
launched new in the last year.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.280
DLP has a support
for detectors which

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.790
are effective at finding common
types of private information,

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.680
and we support custom
detectors which

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.430
enable you to search for
unique business data.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.770
But this new feature,
detection rules,

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.860
allows you to refine the
results found by those detectors

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.830
by modifying their behavior
to reduce noise and increase

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.500
precision.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.600
Let me show you
two quick examples.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.560
In this case, we have a data
set that we're going to be

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.160
de-identifying that has both
company email addresses--

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.960
those owned by my employees--

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.910
and customer email addresses.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:17.230
I don't want to redact my
employee email addresses.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.060
In fact, I'd like to
leave those alone,

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.440
and I don't want them to
show up in findings reports.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.890
By setting up an exclusion
rule, in this case

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:30.630
I'd just tell it to exclude
support@example.com findings.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.180
You can support dictionaries
here or regular expressions so

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.430
that you can exclude these
from your findings and only

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.740
redact your customer
email addresses.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:42.410
Here's a second example.

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:45.560
In this data example, we have
a data set that has names

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.090
that we'd like to redact.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.520
The doctor names aren't
important to protect,

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.880
but the patient names
we need to redact.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.500
Because they both
show up as findings

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.000
in the normal
operating mode, they'd

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.160
both be redacted as equals.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.590
But in this case, I can
boost the likelihood

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:04.100
of my patient names by including
a hot word rule that looks

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.000
for words nearby the name--

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.670
in this case, the word patient--

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:08.870
and I boost the certainty.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.390
And by setting min
likelihood to very likely,

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.953
the only names that will get
redacted are my patient names,

00:06:13.953 --> 00:06:15.620
and the doctor names
will be left alone.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.580
So for a while now we've
supported custom dictionaries,

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.920
which were ideal when
you had a short list

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:28.340
of secretive or sensitive words
that you wanted to scan for,

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.220
but they didn't work if you had
a list of words that changed

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.000
frequently, or if your
list was long, which brings

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.980
us to stored dictionaries.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:39.800
Stored dictionaries
are easy to update

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.290
and they support tens
of millions of entries.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.750
They're great for things
like, as in the picture,

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.430
user ID dictionaries.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:48.260
There's no pattern
or regular expression

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:50.900
I can use to detect for these,
but if you give me a store

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:53.330
dictionary, I can find these
in both your structured and

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:56.307
unstructured data.

00:06:56.307 --> 00:06:57.890
So now that I've
given you the ability

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:01.130
to fine tune your findings,
the next set of features

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:03.110
are about modifying
what you scan.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.150
If you're scanning
BigQuery or Cloud storage,

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.600
scanning all of the content
may not be necessary.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.600
Sometimes a million findings
is no more meaningful

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.690
than one finding, and it's
certainly not always the most

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:21.360
cost effective strategy.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.220
So with this, we've
offered several new ways

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.610
to sample your data.

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:28.720
I'll quickly show two
very simple examples.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:31.920
In this example, we're
scanning a BigQuery table.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:35.040
And I'm going to limit the scan
to select 1,000 rows randomly

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.180
from the table.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.537
Each row is about
equally the same to me,

00:07:39.537 --> 00:07:41.370
and so this allows me
to get a sense of what

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:43.680
type of private information
is in the table.

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.330
I can do the same thing
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:50.800
It works a little differently.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.050
In this case, I
give it a bucket,

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:57.310
and I say select 25% randomly
of the files in this bucket

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.910
but only scan 200
bytes of those.

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:03.483
By doing this quick
check, I can completely--

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:04.900
I can check for
potential problems

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:08.470
without incurring the
costs of exhaustive scans.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:10.300
If I joined this with
scheduled triggers,

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.820
I can spot check my buckets
on a regular cadence

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:16.202
to assure there are no
regressions as new data is

00:08:16.202 --> 00:08:17.285
being added to my buckets.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:21.910
Finally, our
biggest announcement

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.130
we have today to
share with you is

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:28.480
our launch of the beta of
our Cloud DLP in the console.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.580
Here you'll be able to
schedule inspection jobs,

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.659
manage templates, and evaluate
findings, which takes us

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:38.070
to our time to demo,
where Scott will show you

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.159
this UI along with all of
our other new features.

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:40.951
SCOTT ELLIS: Great.

00:08:40.951 --> 00:08:42.080
Thank you, Jordanna.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.240
So I'm going to walk
through a lot of the stuff

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.073
that Jordanna just
talked about and show you

00:08:46.073 --> 00:08:48.890
some examples of using our
Cloud DLP infrastructure

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:50.360
kind of in action.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:53.610
Just to get started,
we'll start with our UI.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:55.850
I'm just going to walk
through creating an inspection

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:59.330
job, which is a very
powerful, but a simple thing

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:00.507
to do in the UI.

00:09:00.507 --> 00:09:02.840
So what we see when we get
here is we see a list of jobs

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:03.800
that we've run.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.560
These are situations we've
gone in and inspected something

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:09.020
like BigQuery or Cloud storage.

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:11.300
We've also got a section
called job triggers.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.120
Those, again, are jobs that
you might trigger or repeat

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:14.990
on a regular basis.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:17.490
So I've got a couple of examples
here of jobs that are going

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:18.657
to run in a regular cadence.

00:09:18.657 --> 00:09:21.560
Maybe you want to run these
daily, weekly, monthly, things

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:22.400
like that.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.650
And you can go in and edit
your templates as well.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:26.900
What I'm going to start off--
we'll just create a job.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:31.370
And let's see--
let's call this--

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:39.012
and we're going to go
look at Cloud storage.

00:09:39.012 --> 00:09:40.470
And I'm just going
to run this job.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:44.310
I'm going to go find a
bucket that we have here,

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.310
which kind of represents a--

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.230
like, a shared bucket
across our company

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:49.860
has a lot of different
data in there.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.170
I simply pick the bucket.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:53.180
I'm going to jump into some
of the advanced configuration

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:54.597
to show you some
of those features

00:09:54.597 --> 00:09:56.720
that Jordanna talked about.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:58.430
First, you can include
an exclude path.

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:00.763
Maybe there's certain file
types you don't want to scan,

00:10:00.763 --> 00:10:02.630
or certain ones you
only want to scan,

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:05.550
but let's go ahead and
jump into sampling here.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:07.840
So I'm going to pick
to do a random sample

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.700
of the first megabyte of
every file in that bucket,

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:16.070
and then we'll just select to
scan about 50% of our files

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:16.850
in that bucket.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:19.040
So with that simple
configuration,

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:20.740
Cloud DLP will take
care of the rest.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:23.000
It will basically go
in, scan that bucket,

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:24.530
randomly sample the files.

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:26.630
When it finds a file
that's in the sample,

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:29.000
it's going to only scan a
random sample of the first--

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.230
of up to one megabyte.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:32.555
So this gives us a good sample.

00:10:32.555 --> 00:10:34.680
And now, we might have
really large files in there,

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:39.130
so this helps us kind
of get that spot check.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:40.550
Now, for detection,
I'm just going

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.530
to have it scan for a couple
of different things here.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:49.560
Let's say we look for credit
card numbers, email addresses.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:50.510
Let's see here.

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:52.923
We'll do social
security numbers.

00:10:52.923 --> 00:10:54.590
And again, these are
the built in types,

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:59.710
so just very simply I can select
them, add them in, and go.

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:01.813
So the next section
here is actions.

00:11:01.813 --> 00:11:03.480
Now, actions are
basically, what happens

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:04.480
when this job completes?

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:06.210
What do I want to do with it?

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:07.883
We can publish a
Pub/Sub notification,

00:11:07.883 --> 00:11:10.050
so you can pick that up and
kind of programmatically

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:11.430
assess the results.

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:13.380
You can save detail
findings to BigQuery.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.290
This is a very
common option to do,

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:17.040
and it's very popular
because you actually

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.890
get the detailed
results of the findings.

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:21.932
You can publish to Cloud
Security Command Center.

00:11:21.932 --> 00:11:23.682
If you're familiar
with that, it basically

00:11:23.682 --> 00:11:25.980
is a way to aggregate a lot
of signals into one place,

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:29.070
or just notify by email
job status, job done,

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:31.167
things like that.

00:11:31.167 --> 00:11:33.250
We can set up a schedule
to run this periodically,

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:35.042
but for this one, I'm
just going to run it.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:40.300
We get the configuration there
in case you want to use that.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:41.695
And then we just create the job.

00:11:41.695 --> 00:11:42.860
So again, it's that easy.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:43.920
This job is going to run.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:45.545
It's going to look
for the three things

00:11:45.545 --> 00:11:48.140
that I specified in that
config, and then we'll

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:51.350
be notified when it's done.

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.980
But what I'm going to do is I'm
going to jump over to a trigger

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:55.480
that we created earlier.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.980
And so, again, we just scanned
kind of the company share.

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:00.910
But I wanted to kind of walk
through another common scenario

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.260
that we see, which is--

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:05.470
let's imagine that your
company has a website.

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:08.200
And you've got assets that power
that website that are public.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:11.480
And you want to host those
in a Cloud storage market.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:15.470
So I'm going to go ahead and
open up this bucket here,

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:17.587
and it's just called
company website.

00:12:17.587 --> 00:12:19.670
And you'll see in that
little indicator at the top

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:22.190
that it's a public bucket,
and we're hosting things

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:25.460
like our images, HTML, CSS,
maybe some videos, things

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.110
that we want to share publicly.

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:29.210
But one concern I have
is I want to make sure

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.520
that no employees go in
and throw sensitive data

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:34.760
in that bucket because
then it would be exposed.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:40.980
So to do that, I've created a
trigger that is going to run,

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:42.980
and I'll just kind of
walk through what that is.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:45.560
So it's going to basically
scan that bucket.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.380
We're basically looking at
the company website bucket.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.500
We're going to scan
it recursively.

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:53.820
And we're going to publish
the findings into BigQuery

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.070
so we can look at the details.

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:55.730
What was found?

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:56.870
Where was it found?

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:59.120
And I'm also including an
option called include quote.

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:00.703
And what that does
is it will actually

00:13:00.703 --> 00:13:03.290
include the quote of the value
because I want to go in and see

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:04.122
what was found.

00:13:04.122 --> 00:13:06.080
And I'm going to lock
down that BigQuery table.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:07.790
This is a table
under my control.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:10.490
So I'm going to put that in
there and lock that down.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.890
And you could run
this every day,

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:17.200
but for this we're just
basically going to run this,

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.387
and you can do a run now.

00:13:19.387 --> 00:13:21.220
So clicking Run Now
here will kick off a job

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:22.637
and just run it right now.

00:13:22.637 --> 00:13:24.220
The other thing that
I'm doing as well

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.500
is I'm only scanning
the difference.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:28.510
So let me show you what
that looks like here.

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:31.480
When you do set
up a periodic run,

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:33.480
you can basically have
it only scan new content.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:35.480
So in this case, because
it's my public website,

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:37.720
I'm doing an exhaustive
scan, but let's say

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:40.150
next week when I run it again,
or tomorrow when I run it

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:42.010
again, I only want to look
at things that have changed

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:42.968
or things that are new.

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:49.730
So the first time I ran this--

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:53.260
let me pull up that job here.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:56.285
The first time I ran this,
I ran on the public website

00:13:56.285 --> 00:13:57.160
and found two things.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:59.053
I found email address
and phone number.

00:13:59.053 --> 00:14:00.720
Now, this wasn't too
shocking because it

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:01.595
was a public website.

00:14:01.595 --> 00:14:03.343
We expect to find
something on there.

00:14:03.343 --> 00:14:05.510
So let me go ahead and look
at what that looks like.

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:08.830
So I'm going to jump into the
detailed findings in BigQuery.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.000
And what this does is
it takes us directly

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.000
into the findings for that job.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:20.082
And I have a query
here I'm going to run.

00:14:20.082 --> 00:14:21.540
And as you see in
this query, we're

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:23.482
basically just
selecting a few fields.

00:14:23.482 --> 00:14:25.440
And we're going to limit
it to that one job run

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:27.898
because I want to show what
that baseline scan looked like.

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:34.277
And let's see here-- just
scroll this up a little bit.

00:14:34.277 --> 00:14:36.110
And as you'll see here,
we found two things.

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:38.690
We found a company phone number,
and we found a company email

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:39.190
address.

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:40.250
Not a big deal.

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:40.750
Right?

00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:41.660
That's totally fine.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:43.552
We're happy with that.

00:14:43.552 --> 00:14:45.760
They're on that particular
web page, so that's great.

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:46.940
That's our baseline scan.

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:49.023
So what I wanted to make
sure of is that something

00:14:49.023 --> 00:14:51.260
didn't change over time.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:55.410
Let's jump back, and let's
go back to that trigger.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:15:00.290
So when I ran this
trigger again,

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:02.160
the next time nothing was there.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.410
So we just got, again, no
results, nothing scanned,

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:05.250
no new files.

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:12.570
But then when I ran
it the third time,

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:15.530
it looks like
something was in there.

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:17.397
A bunch of data
has now showed up.

00:15:17.397 --> 00:15:18.230
We saw a huge spike.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:22.730
We saw 10,000 email addresses,
10,000 social security numbers,

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:25.310
and we also saw this thing--
a hit for a custom type

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:26.980
called a custom designed doc.

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:28.820
So again, that's very alarming.

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:31.070
Clearly, this doesn't sound
like something that should

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:33.000
be on our public website.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:37.720
And if we dive in and look
at the findings here--

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:40.670
let's just build
a query live here.

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:43.089
And I'm just going
to look at star.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.000
And as we see here, we're
actually seeing the raw data.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:53.545
Now, this is all fake
data that we made up,

00:15:53.545 --> 00:15:55.670
but in your real scenario,
this is the actual data.

00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:57.838
You can see what it is.

00:15:57.838 --> 00:15:58.880
You can see where it was.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:01.430
It looks like somebody
uploaded in this temp folder

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:05.910
an export CSV file that clearly
had some sensitive data in it.

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:07.970
We also had that one
design doc, so we

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:09.020
want to try to find
out where that is.

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:10.645
Now, what I'm doing
here is I'm looking

00:16:10.645 --> 00:16:13.155
at the raw SQL in
BigQuery, and you

00:16:13.155 --> 00:16:14.530
can run any kind
of analytics you

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:16.760
would run in SQL directly here.

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:19.580
What that also means is
that you can use Data

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:21.650
Studio to visualize this data.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:23.570
So I'm going to open
up an audit report

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:27.860
that we built that was built
on top of that SQL query--

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:29.540
that SQL database--
and I've just

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:32.180
done some very, very
simple visualization here.

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:35.300
We see we've got some
jobs that have run.

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:38.300
And I wanted to look
at this kind of results

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:41.340
here and narrow down where
we saw that design doc.

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.400
So I'm just going do a
very simple filter here.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:47.020
And if we see, there's
a file in that bucket

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:48.950
that looks like it had
a hit for a design doc.

00:16:48.950 --> 00:16:51.690
So let's go see what
that looks like.

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:53.062
So I'm in my public bucket here.

00:16:53.062 --> 00:16:54.020
Go to that temp folder.

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:56.300
We see that export folder
that had that CSV with all

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:58.020
the personal data.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:00.760
We see a screenshot folder
with a bunch of screenshots.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.682
And if I open up
this one screenshot,

00:17:02.682 --> 00:17:04.099
what we're seeing
here is it looks

00:17:04.099 --> 00:17:07.250
like somebody took a
screenshot of our design doc

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:08.692
and uploaded it to the website.

00:17:08.692 --> 00:17:11.150
And we have a custom type that's
looking for our design doc

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:12.030
identifiers.

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:14.540
So we OCR this doc
as part of that scan,

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:18.140
found our design doc
identifier in that doc,

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:19.369
and flagged it as a hit.

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:22.640
Again, that was a custom design
doc detector that we built.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:25.400
So very simply, whether
it's through BigQuery,

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:27.349
running SQL, or a
simple audit report,

00:17:27.349 --> 00:17:30.560
I'm able to get in and
find that information

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:34.010
and identify, again, that change
between that baseline scan

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.360
and the next scan.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:38.960
Now, I'm going to go
back to one slide here.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:41.880
Now, clearly, if you
have a public bucket,

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:43.670
finding that data--
monitoring for that

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.078
data-- is very crucial.

00:17:45.078 --> 00:17:46.620
But you also might
want to prevent it

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:49.320
from happening in
the first place.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:51.840
Me finding out about it the
day after or the week after,

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:53.650
again, it's already there.

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:56.260
So you can also use
DLP to do things

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:59.670
like scan that data before it
gets moved into the bucket.

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:01.290
This is a reference
architecture.

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:02.130
There's a link at the bottom--

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.088
I know it's a little bit
long, but you can find

00:18:04.088 --> 00:18:05.310
this on our website as well--

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:06.930
that basically takes
you through using

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:11.460
DLP and what we call kind of
a staged bucket approach where

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.770
before you move data
into a public bucket,

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:15.630
you would scan it
with DLP and have

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.360
the results of that job using
Cloud Functions actually

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:21.767
move the file in to the
public bucket or reject it.

00:18:21.767 --> 00:18:23.850
So that's another way you
can apply this technique

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:27.645
to prevent the data from
getting in in the first place.

00:18:27.645 --> 00:18:29.520
So now we're going to
go to a different demo.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:33.210
We're going to go to an
example of using DLP--

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:34.710
we're going to use
the UI as well--

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:36.100
but we're going to apply it
to do something different.

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:37.850
We're going to look
at BigQuery, and we're

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:42.280
going to try to use DLP to
basically classify a table.

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:44.780
So to get started, let's
go look at that table.

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:48.340
I'm going to close a
couple of tabs here.

00:18:48.340 --> 00:18:51.430
And in this example,
we have a data engineer

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:53.200
who uploaded a CSV file.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.120
It didn't have a
header row, or it just

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:56.650
had fields one through seven.

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:58.070
We don't know what's in there.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.540
Now, with a single table,
you can look at the table

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:01.810
and you can figure
out what's in there.

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:03.700
But imagine if you had
thousands of these tables

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:05.992
and you wanted to set up a
process where you could scan

00:19:05.992 --> 00:19:07.630
it, look for certain things--

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:09.692
sensitive data-- and
use those findings

00:19:09.692 --> 00:19:10.900
to try to classify the table.

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:13.413
And that's what we're
going to do today.

00:19:13.413 --> 00:19:14.830
So the first thing
I'm going to do

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:20.510
is I'm going to go back
to our DLP triggers page.

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:23.090
So I've created this
trigger and I'll

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:25.250
walk through what
that looks like here.

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:29.640
So we're basically using
this trigger to scan a table.

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:31.040
I'll show it from here.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:33.620
We're basically going to be
scanning that table that I just

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:34.670
showed.

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:36.260
Now, it's a big
table, so we don't

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:37.730
need to scan the whole thing.

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:40.560
We set it up to basically
do a random 10% sample,

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:43.490
and we're going to use those
results and try to collect--

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:46.580
kind of aggregate then to guess
what the classification is

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:49.070
of that table.

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:51.005
And let's go back here.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:02.720
And I wanted to load
the job that we ran.

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:05.110
So when we look at the job
results, as you see here,

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:07.070
we found a lot of
different things.

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:09.760
So this is just giving us
a summary of the data--

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:13.330
credit cards, emails, IP
addresses, VIN number, SSN,

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:14.360
phone number.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:16.032
We also set up two custom types.

00:20:16.032 --> 00:20:18.490
Now, these were types similar--
if you remember the example

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:21.650
that Jordanna gave for a
large custom dictionary.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:23.572
What we did is we
set up our user IDs

00:20:23.572 --> 00:20:25.030
and we built two
big dictionaries--

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:27.483
one for our US users and
one for our European users.

00:20:27.483 --> 00:20:28.900
And in this scan,
it looks like we

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:34.532
found about 7,000 examples of
US users and 3,000 of EU users,

00:20:34.532 --> 00:20:36.990
and that's kind of showing the
power of these dictionaries.

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:39.282
Those dictionaries themselves
had millions and millions

00:20:39.282 --> 00:20:42.920
of user data in them.

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:45.580
In this case, we just did a
sample scan of 10,000 rows,

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:50.810
and so we found about a good
70/30 percentage of that.

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:51.310
OK.

00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:52.893
So now what we're
going to do is we're

00:20:52.893 --> 00:20:55.540
going to jump in and let's look
at the actual BigQuery table.

00:20:58.290 --> 00:21:00.390
Let's say-- I'm going
to go back here,

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:03.970
and let's just go straight
from here from the findings.

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:06.080
There we go.

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:09.040
So again, in that trigger,
what we did is we set this up.

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:11.020
We wrote the detailed
findings in BigQuery,

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:13.062
and now we're going to go
in and look at BigQuery

00:21:13.062 --> 00:21:14.800
and see if we can use
that to understand

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:19.115
what's in that table.

00:21:19.115 --> 00:21:19.615
OK.

00:21:19.615 --> 00:21:26.022
So I'm going to load a
query here and run it.

00:21:26.022 --> 00:21:27.480
So what this query
is doing is it's

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:30.083
taking the detailed
findings and it's basically

00:21:30.083 --> 00:21:31.250
collecting all the findings.

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:33.750
You're going to get one
row for every finding,

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.908
but it's just doing a simple
collect those per field ID

00:21:36.908 --> 00:21:39.450
because you're going to get the
field name, the table column,

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:40.780
all that stuff in there.

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:42.780
And as we see here,
we've basically

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:45.300
done that to apply
that to each field,

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:47.140
and we see that there's
a good pattern here.

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:49.960
The first column was
our user ID column.

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:54.240
Had, again, that mix of 70/30
of US and European users.

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:57.010
We see a VIN number,
email address, IP address,

00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:57.870
phone number--

00:21:57.870 --> 00:21:59.790
not completely, so not
all of them in there

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:01.530
hit as a phone number--

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:03.100
SSN, and credit card number.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:05.760
So again, we're kind of walking
through this process manually,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:08.100
but they did here is if you
had thousands of tables,

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:10.392
you could go in and apply
this sort of programmatically

00:22:10.392 --> 00:22:13.140
and say, I want to apply this
to every new table coming in,

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:14.490
validate the schema.

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:17.130
Or in the case of this one,
it really didn't have one.

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:19.350
Identify if there's any
sensitive data in there,

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:25.064
and then be able to
get results like this.

00:22:25.064 --> 00:22:25.900
OK.

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:31.467
And so that's applying our
large custom dictionaries,

00:22:31.467 --> 00:22:33.300
and also applying kind
of a unique use of it

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:35.680
to basically go in and
classify a BigQuery table.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:36.540
The next thing
we're going to do is

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:39.270
we're going to show how to apply
some of our exclusion rules,

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:42.030
and we're going to start off by
showing how you would use those

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:43.760
during a large
inspection job like what

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:45.210
we've been using the UI for.

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:46.200
And then right after
we're going to show

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:48.240
how you can apply that
same template that we

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:51.190
create in a real time scenario.

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:56.220
So first, I want to go
back to that first bucket

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:58.870
that we scanned when we
kind of created our own job.

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:02.020
So in that bucket, this is
kind of our company share--

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:03.230
a lot of stuff's in there--

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:05.730
there's a development folder
that somebody put a lot of logs

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:06.450
in there.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:07.380
Now, these are supposed
to be development

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:09.270
logs that the whole
company can look at

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:10.980
and only have test data in them.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:13.440
But again, we're concerned
something might leak in.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:16.837
We want to set up a process
to monitor for that data.

00:23:16.837 --> 00:23:18.420
And so what we're
going to do is we're

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:22.430
going to go into our data
loss prevention section.

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:27.180
I have created a job
trigger for this.

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:32.790
And first, I think what
we're going to do, though,

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:33.995
is we are going to create--

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:40.150
we're going to basically
do a sample scan.

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:43.870
So let me show you
the scan that we ran.

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:46.060
And what we wanted to do
is, before we exhaustively

00:23:46.060 --> 00:23:48.148
scanned all of that
data, we wanted

00:23:48.148 --> 00:23:50.440
to just do a sample to kind
of see the things we found.

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:52.565
Again, we expect to find
a lot of data that we kind

00:23:52.565 --> 00:23:54.850
of don't care about-- all
of our test accounts--

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:56.570
so we want to see
what that looks like.

00:23:56.570 --> 00:23:58.420
So what I did was
I created a job.

00:23:58.420 --> 00:24:00.970
We ran this job, only
looked for email addresses.

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:03.020
And as you see,
even in that sample,

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:04.820
we found 21,000 email addresses.

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:05.875
These are log files.

00:24:05.875 --> 00:24:08.250
Not a big surprise that there's
a lot of emails in there,

00:24:08.250 --> 00:24:10.406
but we want to get a sense
of what these look like.

00:24:10.406 --> 00:24:12.906
So again, right from here we're
going to jump into BigQuery.

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:18.130
Let's run a query here.

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:20.220
And we will see
what they look like.

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:26.470
So as we see, a
lot of emails are

00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:28.600
coming in across all
of our log files,

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:31.075
but they all have
this testdlp.com email

00:24:31.075 --> 00:24:32.950
address in there, which
is our test accounts.

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:34.220
So we're good.

00:24:34.220 --> 00:24:35.540
We're happy with that.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:37.610
But again, if I run
this job every day

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:40.553
and it comes up with 21,000
findings, or 30,000 findings,

00:24:40.553 --> 00:24:42.970
it's not going to be very
useful for me when most of those

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:44.265
are ones I don't care about.

00:24:44.265 --> 00:24:46.390
So what we're going to do
is we're going to go back

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:49.110
and we're going to create
an exclusion template.

00:24:49.110 --> 00:24:51.027
So I'm going to show you
what that looks like.

00:24:51.027 --> 00:24:53.757
We're going to show
a code sample here.

00:24:53.757 --> 00:24:55.840
What I'm doing here is I'm
using our API Explorer.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:57.215
I'm going to pull
up and show you

00:24:57.215 --> 00:25:00.540
kind of in detail
what that looks like

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:01.870
and how simple it is.

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:06.210
So in this example, we're
showing the JSON representation

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:07.032
of a template.

00:25:07.032 --> 00:25:08.490
And we simply turned
on three types

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:10.980
that we cared about--
email address, credit card,

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:12.880
and social security number.

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:14.910
And at the bottom,
we created a rule--

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:18.760
an exclusion rule-- that simply
says, if you see testdlp.com

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:22.170
in the result of an email
address, partial match,

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:23.212
just ignore it.

00:25:23.212 --> 00:25:24.670
That way the prefix,
the user name,

00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:25.920
whatever it is, we don't care.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:27.810
It'll match on the
suffix and we'll be good.

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:32.990
I'm going to leave that one up.

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:34.262
Go to my job triggers page.

00:25:34.262 --> 00:25:35.720
So that's the
template that we want

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:38.108
to use now for our
ongoing periodic scan,

00:25:38.108 --> 00:25:39.650
and that way the
findings that we get

00:25:39.650 --> 00:25:41.510
will be meaningful to us.

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:45.040
So we set those
up in a template--

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:45.700
in a trigger.

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:52.560
We are going to use this
email exclude template

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:54.780
that we just created,
that we just showed you.

00:25:54.780 --> 00:25:57.197
We're going to do a exhaustive
scan now because, again, we

00:25:57.197 --> 00:25:58.613
want to make sure
that there isn't

00:25:58.613 --> 00:26:00.150
that one email
address or two email

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.173
addresses leaking into our logs.

00:26:02.173 --> 00:26:04.590
So we did the sample scan to
figure out what was going on,

00:26:04.590 --> 00:26:05.840
get a sense of what was there.

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:09.870
Now we did the exhaustive
scan to search for everything.

00:26:09.870 --> 00:26:11.068
And let's see what happened.

00:26:11.068 --> 00:26:12.360
We're going to open up the job.

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:17.110
And it looks like we
found two email addresses.

00:26:17.110 --> 00:26:18.527
So this is actually
useful, right?

00:26:18.527 --> 00:26:21.330
We went from 21,000 in
a small sample scan,

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:26.040
and now we scanned
about 626 megabytes

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:27.670
of log files that
are up there, and we

00:26:27.670 --> 00:26:28.780
found two email addresses.

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:31.113
So these are probably ones
we really care about and want

00:26:31.113 --> 00:26:31.727
to go look at.

00:26:31.727 --> 00:26:33.560
So I'm going to jump
into the findings here.

00:26:41.096 --> 00:26:42.050
Let's see here.

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:50.790
And just open up
this saved query.

00:26:50.790 --> 00:26:52.290
What I'm doing here
is I'm basically

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:55.380
looking at just the results here
for that last scan that we did.

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:57.970
And again, it's
sample data here,

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:00.570
but these are two that are
not part of our test set.

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:02.700
It's the same email
address showed up twice

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:05.430
in this one log--

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:06.930
prod test logs five.

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:10.620
So clearly that prod test logs
five maybe wasn't a dev log

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:12.310
and shouldn't have
been in there.

00:27:12.310 --> 00:27:14.070
And again, we've been able
to very easily identify

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:16.403
that using exclusion lists,
weeding out all the stuff we

00:27:16.403 --> 00:27:18.030
didn't care about.

00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:24.430
Now, to do that again, we
used this exclusion rule.

00:27:24.430 --> 00:27:26.530
We applied it when we
did this large scan

00:27:26.530 --> 00:27:28.660
where we basically
went in, scanned

00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:31.150
600 and something
megabytes of data,

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:32.715
applied it in a large operation.

00:27:32.715 --> 00:27:34.090
But that template
is very useful.

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:36.470
Again, that holds
the configuration

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:38.470
of what you care about,
what you're looking for.

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:39.970
What we're going
to do next is we're

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:43.227
going to use that
template and we're

00:27:43.227 --> 00:27:44.810
going to apply it
in another scenario.

00:27:44.810 --> 00:27:46.560
We're going to show
how that same template

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:49.910
and same exclusion rule
can be used in real time.

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:53.540
To do that, I'm going to pull
open a sample application

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:56.660
that we built,
and I'm just going

00:27:56.660 --> 00:27:59.267
to load-- this is a live
classification demo that we

00:27:59.267 --> 00:28:00.100
built in App Engine.

00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:02.920
What it's doing is it's taking
the data that's on the left box

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:05.470
and automatically applying
one of our DLP inspection

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:08.620
and transforms, and then putting
the output in the right box.

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:11.980
So what we see here
is a sample chat

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:14.845
where we've got one of our
agents talking to a customer,

00:28:14.845 --> 00:28:16.720
and we simply said, look
for a bunch of stuff

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:18.320
and just redact it
when you find it.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:20.490
So on the right side, you
see all the emails have

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:23.032
been replaced with
email address, SSNs

00:28:23.032 --> 00:28:24.490
with the US social
security number,

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:25.900
and credit cards replaced.

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:28.030
But again, we want to
apply that template now

00:28:28.030 --> 00:28:30.640
because we didn't really want
to hide our agent's name.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:31.930
We want to know who that is.

00:28:31.930 --> 00:28:34.510
And in this case,
the james@testdlp.com

00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:38.780
was hidden because we just
treated it as an email address.

00:28:38.780 --> 00:28:40.940
So what I'm going to do is
I'm going to go in here.

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:43.060
I'm going to load this template.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:49.450
This is the-- hold on a second.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:49.950
Let's see.

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:51.692
Template name.

00:28:51.692 --> 00:28:53.900
I'm going to take this
template name that we created.

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:01.310
And in our live kind
of real time demo,

00:29:01.310 --> 00:29:03.310
we have the ability just
to paste in a template.

00:29:03.310 --> 00:29:05.602
So we're showing-- we're not
going to go edit the code,

00:29:05.602 --> 00:29:07.760
we're just simply going to
have the application use

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.420
a different template.

00:29:09.420 --> 00:29:10.670
I'm going to paste it in here.

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:16.800
And so now if I go back to this
and update it, what you see

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:20.243
is the right side refreshed as
soon as I typed something in,

00:29:20.243 --> 00:29:21.660
and now we are no
longer excluding

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:23.190
the james@testdlp.com.

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:25.980
So again, just showing that
that one same template that we

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:28.710
copied straight from the
inspect job, applied here,

00:29:28.710 --> 00:29:31.260
you can use it in all of your
kind of pipelines and workflows

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.480
and have that consistency,
and not have to go in

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:34.980
and change code.

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:37.820
Just simply reference
a different resource.

00:29:37.820 --> 00:29:40.168
And again, that's using
our exclusion rules.

00:29:40.168 --> 00:29:42.210
There's a lot more power
that you can do as well,

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:44.460
creating your own custom
types, exclusion rules,

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:46.060
combining those together.

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:48.570
All of that, again, no matter
how simple or complex it is

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:50.623
can be saved as a
template and then reused

00:29:50.623 --> 00:29:51.540
across your workflows.

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:54.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

